<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lewis and Clark Expedition</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–<a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Corps of Discovery</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lewis and Clark Expedition was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army expedition led by Meriwether Lewis and William Clark from 1804 to 1806 to explore the newly acquired Louisiana Purchase and find a route to the Pacific. Aided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshone">Shoshone</a> woman Sacagawea, it travelled up the Missouri River and across the Rocky Mountains to the Pacific coast and back, recording the land and its peoples, plants and animals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lewis-and-clark-expedition.jpg" alt="Lewis and Clark Expedition" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lewis_and_Clark.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> commissioned the expedition shortly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> of 1803. He wished to find the most direct and practicable water communication across the continent for commerce, to learn the resources of the new territory and to declare <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> sovereignty over the Native American peoples along the Missouri. Alexander Mackenzie had reached the Pacific coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> in 1793, and his account of 1801 told Jefferson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s intent to control the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> of the Columbia River. Scientific work was a secondary aim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Clark and about thirty others left Camp Dubois, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, on 14 May 1804 and went up the Missouri River. Lewis joined them at St. Charles, Missouri. The Corps may have had as many as 45 members, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, a Black man enslaved by Clark. Sergeant Charles Floyd died on 20 August 1804, apparently of acute appendicitis, and was the only member to die.</p><p>The party crossed the Continental Divide near Lemhi Pass and reached the Columbia River. It sighted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> on 7 November 1805. Short of food, the party voted on 24 November to move to the south side of the river, and both Sacagawea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> took part in the vote. There the men built Fort Clatsop and spent the winter. The return journey began on 23 March 1806 and ended at St. Louis on 23 September.</p><p>Sacagawea, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshone">Shoshone</a> woman, joined the expedition with her husband, Toussaint Charbonneau, and gave birth to a son in February 1805. Scholars say she was not the guide but was important as an interpreter, and the sight of a woman with an infant helped to give the impression of a peaceful mission. The expedition relied on the help of two dozen Native American nations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Corps did not find a continuous waterway to the Pacific, but it located a trail from the upper Missouri to the Columbia. It drew about 140 maps and described more than 200 new plant and animal species. Its medals, flags and proclamations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> were legal steps toward claiming title to the land of each indigenous nation under the discovery doctrine. The maps allowed further exploration and settlement.</p><p>All the other members received double pay and land, but Clark refused to free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, who received nothing and was still enslaved ten years after the expedition. His later life is disputed. Lewis and Clark County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a> and the Lewis and Clark River in Oregon are named after the two leaders.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lewis and Clark Expedition” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the <b>Lewis and Clark Expedition</b> met the Sicangu in 1804, warriors seized a boat’s cable and both sides readied their weapons, until the Sicangu leader Black Buffalo let go and invited the Americans upriver under Sicangu escort.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lakota-people">Lakota people · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
